---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import samples from '../../data/samples.json';
const files=[{name:'Canon EOS R6 Mark II',sample:samples[0],mode:'Full-frame normal RAW (CR3)'},{name:'Sony A7 III',sample:samples[1],mode:'14-bit compressed RAW (ARW)'},{name:'Nikon D750',sample:samples[2],mode:'14-bit lossless compressed RAW (NEF)'}];
const format=(n:number)=>(n/1e6).toFixed(2);
const paths=[['/raw-file-size/','RAW file size'],['/compressed-vs-uncompressed-raw/','RAW compression'],['/raw-plus-jpeg/','RAW + JPEG']];
---
<Layout title="Compressed vs Uncompressed RAW: What Changes?" description="Compare lossless, lossy and uncompressed RAW workflows, with original source-file measurements from Canon, Sony and Nikon cameras."><article class="wrap prose"><p class="eyebrow">RAW COMPRESSION GUIDE</p><h1>Compressed vs uncompressed RAW</h1><p class="lead">Uncompressed RAW preserves recorded values without a size-reduction codec. Lossless compression restores the recorded values exactly after decoding; lossy RAW reduces data in ways that may not be perfectly reversible. Choose based on your camera's modes, workflow and editing needs.</p><h2>Three RAW recording approaches</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>What it means</th><th>Practical trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Uncompressed</th><td>Sensor data stored without RAW compression</td><td>Often larger files and simpler size planning</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lossless compressed</th><td>Decoded values reproduce the original recorded values</td><td>Saves space without lossy changes to recorded values</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lossy compressed</th><td>Discarded or transformed information cannot always be reconstructed exactly</td><td>Smaller files may involve edge cases in extreme edits</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Which modes are actually represented here?</h2><p>These source records document particular modes. We do not have matched uncompressed and compressed captures of the same scene and camera, so this table cannot prove a numerical compression percentage.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Single original files measured in decimal megabytes; not camera averages</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Camera</th><th scope="col">Capture mode</th><th scope="col">Measured RAW</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead><tbody>{files.map(f=><tr><th scope="row"><a href={`/${f.sample.slug}-raw-vs-jpeg/`}>{f.name}</a></th><td>{f.mode}</td><td>{format(f.sample.rawBytes)} MB</td><td><a href={f.sample.url}>Original CC0 file ↗</a></td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div><h2>Canon C-RAW versus RAW</h2><p>Canon offers C-RAW on supported EOS cameras as a compact alternative to standard RAW. Our EOS R6 Mark II source is labeled normal full-frame RAW; without a matched C-RAW original, we do not claim an exact saving for this model. Test your own high-contrast scenes before choosing a smaller format for important jobs.</p><h2>Sony compressed versus uncompressed ARW</h2><p>Our Sony A7 III example is identified as 14-bit compressed ARW. It is a valid file-size observation for that capture but not an uncompressed ARW benchmark. Availability and behavior of RAW recording modes differ by Sony body; check the camera's actual menu and manual.</p><h2>Nikon lossless compressed NEF</h2><p>Our Nikon D750 example is labeled 14-bit lossless compressed NEF. This does not establish how much smaller it is than a same-scene uncompressed capture. Different bit depths and other NEF modes require separate measurements.</p><h2>When should you prefer lossless compression?</h2><p>If your camera supports it and your editor reads the files correctly, lossless compression can reduce storage without changing the decoded recorded values. Uncompressed may be appropriate for specific software compatibility or workflow constraints. For lossy modes, evaluate the settings with your own editing and burst-shooting requirements.</p></article><section class="wrap prose compact"><h2>Related guides and tools</h2><ul>{paths.map(([href,label])=><li><a href={href}>{label}</a></li>)}</ul><p><a href="/cameras/">Measured camera index</a> · <a href="/memory-card-calculator/">Memory card calculator</a> · <a href="/methodology/">Sources and methods</a> · <a href="/">Compare RAW and JPEG</a></p></section></Layout>